1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,400
Right now I’m standing outside the injection room at a pediatric clinic.

2
00:00:03,467 --> 00:00:10,600
The kid is the one getting the shot, but it’s the two of us who feel the sting. Current score: Dad 1, Mom 1.

3
00:00:10,667 --> 00:00:13,400
"Sir, this shot can’t stop colds."

4
00:00:13,467 --> 00:00:15,700
Yes. That was the first point.

5
00:00:15,767 --> 00:00:18,167
Let’s rewind ten minutes.

6
00:00:18,733 --> 00:00:26,367
It was October, flu-shot day for kids. The waiting room was packed with children rolling up their sleeves.

7
00:00:26,433 --> 00:00:30,567
Mom joined us straight from work, with a medicine bag tucked in her work bag.

8
00:00:30,633 --> 00:00:33,400
After staring at the poster on the wall for a while, my kid asked.

9
00:00:33,467 --> 00:00:37,000
"Dad, if I get the flu shot, will I stop catching colds?"

10
00:00:37,067 --> 00:00:38,933
"Flu is just a strong cold, right?"

11
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:45,733
"Of course! Flu is the strongest kind of cold. Stop the strongest one and the rest get blocked too."

12
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:48,267
It sounded pretty convincing. To my ears, anyway.

13
00:00:48,333 --> 00:00:52,767
Just then, a nurse holding an injection tray was standing right beside me.

14
00:00:52,833 --> 00:00:57,533
"Sir, this shot can’t stop colds. It only stops the flu."

15
00:00:57,600 --> 00:00:59,733
Sting. Point logged.

16
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:02,300
"Then what about a cold shot?"

17
00:01:02,367 --> 00:01:04,667
"There’s no shot for colds."

18
00:01:05,233 --> 00:01:07,100
This time my kid turned to look at Mom.

19
00:01:07,167 --> 00:01:09,833
"Mom, what do you do when you catch a cold?"

20
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:13,233
"I take medicine. Medicine makes you better, right?"

21
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:17,567
Mom was confident. The nurse smiled.

22
00:01:18,133 --> 00:01:28,067
"Ma’am, cold medicine can ease things like a runny nose or a cough for a while. But it can’t cure a cold. And antibiotics don’t work on colds either."

23
00:01:28,133 --> 00:01:29,900
Sting. Point logged.

24
00:01:30,467 --> 00:01:33,433
"Then what makes a cold go away?"

25
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:35,300
"Your body. Our own bodies."

26
00:01:35,867 --> 00:01:40,333
I slipped behind a teddy bear as tall as me in the corner of the waiting room and turned on my phone.

27
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:44,700
The creature called Dad, when cornered, will search even from behind a bear.

28
00:01:44,767 --> 00:01:49,267
To be fair, it was easy to mix up. Even the name sounds like a strong cold.

29
00:01:49,333 --> 00:01:53,267
Both come in winter, both make you cough, both give you a fever.

30
00:01:53,333 --> 00:01:57,800
So today, forget which one is stronger. Let’s look at just one thing.

31
00:01:57,867 --> 00:01:59,133
Who causes it?

32
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:01,667
"Next child, please come in!"

33
00:02:01,733 --> 00:02:03,733
The teddy bear toppled over.

34
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:06,967
First, what I read on the walk to the injection room.

35
00:02:07,033 --> 00:02:15,500
A cold isn’t the name of a single illness. It’s a catch-all word for the viruses that get into your nose and throat and give you a runny nose and a sore throat.

36
00:02:15,567 --> 00:02:18,400
There are more than two hundred of those viruses.

37
00:02:18,467 --> 00:02:24,767
About half are viruses called rhinoviruses, and those alone come in over a hundred and fifty kinds.

38
00:02:24,833 --> 00:02:36,200
These guests mostly get in when a virus-covered hand touches your nose, mouth or eyes. That’s why washing your hands is the easiest way to lock the door.

39
00:02:36,267 --> 00:02:45,033
Colds usually creep in: a runny nose, sneezing, a scratchy throat, little or no fever. And within a week or two most go away on their own.

40
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:53,700
That’s also why there’s no cold shot. With hundreds of culprits, and a different crowd each year, there’s no way to pick which one to block.

41
00:02:54,267 --> 00:03:00,000
Now, the flu. Starting with the name: in English, “influenza.”

42
00:03:00,067 --> 00:03:02,433
In Italian, it means “influence.”

43
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:08,200
Long ago, Italians believed an illness that spread to everyone at once was the influence of the stars.

44
00:03:08,267 --> 00:03:15,533
In 1743, an outbreak that began in Italy spread across Europe, and the name entered English. Shortened, it became “flu.”

45
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:20,133
The real culprit behind the star-blamed illness would not be caught until 190 years later.

46
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:28,833
Britain, 1933. Researchers filter the water used to rinse a flu patient’s throat and put it in a ferret’s nose.

47
00:03:28,900 --> 00:03:33,300
The ferret caught the flu just like a person. Sneezing and all.

48
00:03:33,367 --> 00:03:39,300
And so, for the first time, the culprit behind the flu was caught, and it was one: the influenza virus.

49
00:03:39,867 --> 00:03:47,133
One culprit means you can make a shot aimed at just that one. That’s the flu shot my kid is getting today.

50
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:52,333
But this one is a bit sneaky. Every year it changes its outfit a little.

51
00:03:52,400 --> 00:04:01,900
So every year the World Health Organization picks which outfits it expects this winter, and new vaccines are made to match. That’s why we get a flu shot every year.

52
00:04:02,467 --> 00:04:08,067
In the corner of the poster it said “three-valent.” What it means is the number of compartments in a lunchbox.

53
00:04:08,133 --> 00:04:14,433
The flu comes with two type-A viruses and two type-B lines, four compartments to fill. That was the four-valent vaccine.

54
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:20,533
But one of the type-B lines has not been found anywhere in the world since March 2020.

55
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:27,633
So in 2023 the World Health Organization said that compartment can be dropped. One fewer compartment: three-valent.

56
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:36,800
It didn’t get weaker. The ones that no longer show up were removed, and only the ones that will come were packed. This year’s free vaccination in Korea is the three-compartment kind too, and it now covers kids up to age fourteen.

57
00:04:37,367 --> 00:04:40,667
But why does it have to be winter? Mom asked.

58
00:04:40,733 --> 00:04:44,233
"Isn’t it because it’s cold in winter?"

59
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:50,067
I thought so too, but no. Cold doesn’t create viruses.

60
00:04:50,133 --> 00:04:58,067
Instead, winter air gets very dry. In dry air, flu viruses survive and float around for a long time in tiny droplets, especially when a room’s humidity drops below forty percent.

61
00:04:58,133 --> 00:05:07,467
There’s also research suggesting that breathing cold air cools the inside of your nose and weakens its defense against viruses. Though scientists are still studying it.

62
00:05:07,533 --> 00:05:15,900
On top of that, windows are shut and everyone gathers indoors, so it’s easy to breathe each other’s air. That’s why opening a window now and then is an easy defense.

63
00:05:16,467 --> 00:05:30,967
You’re probably wondering about COVID-19 too. Coronaviruses are a big family, and a few of them are cold guests. But what causes COVID-19 is another virus with its own name. The flu shot can’t stop it, and it has its own vaccine.

64
00:05:31,033 --> 00:05:36,267
And the flu arrives differently. Not creeping in, but suddenly.

65
00:05:36,333 --> 00:05:43,033
Your temperature shoots up, your head hurts, your whole body aches, you shiver. Enough that even getting up is hard.

66
00:05:43,100 --> 00:05:49,433
Most people get better with rest, but young children and older adults can develop other illnesses like pneumonia, so extra care is needed.

67
00:05:49,500 --> 00:05:58,100
And figuring out exactly whether it’s a cold or the flu, as we learned last time, is up to the person who decides the illness: the doctor.

68
00:05:58,667 --> 00:06:00,733
So, to sum up:

69
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:02,867
A cold is hundreds of guests,

70
00:06:02,933 --> 00:06:04,433
the flu is just one,

71
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:07,400
and cold medicine only eases the misery.

72
00:06:07,467 --> 00:06:11,200
The flu isn’t a stronger cold. It’s an illness with a different culprit.

73
00:06:12,633 --> 00:06:18,167
I sat my kid on the chair in the injection room, held their hand, and answered again what I had gotten wrong.

74
00:06:18,233 --> 00:06:22,633
"Remember I said the flu is the strongest cold? That was wrong."

75
00:06:22,700 --> 00:06:30,167
"“Cold” is the name for little guests that come into your nose and throat. There are hundreds of kinds, so nobody can make one shot to stop them all."

76
00:06:30,233 --> 00:06:35,333
"The flu is different. Just one culprit comes. So a shot that blocks only that one can be made."

77
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:40,400
"And since it changes outfits every year, you get a new shot every year."

78
00:06:40,467 --> 00:06:47,167
"Today’s shot can’t stop colds. But it helps you catch the flu less, and hurt less if you do."

79
00:06:47,233 --> 00:06:57,500
"Cold medicine only eases things a little. It’s your body that gets rid of the cold. And before taking any medicine, always ask a doctor or a pharmacist."

80
00:06:57,567 --> 00:07:03,167
"And if you suddenly get a high fever and your whole body aches, you show a doctor."

81
00:07:03,233 --> 00:07:08,633
My kid nodded, squeezed their eyes shut, and took the shot without crying.

82
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:10,400
"Well done holding on."

83
00:07:10,467 --> 00:07:17,167
With a bandage on, my kid pulled a piece of paper from a pocket. It was the temperature note I wrote that night last time.

84
00:07:17,733 --> 00:07:21,767
"Dad should get one too. If Dad gets sick, who writes down my temperature?"

85
00:07:21,833 --> 00:07:24,567
"I’ll write it down. So roll up your sleeve too."

86
00:07:25,133 --> 00:07:26,933
I rolled up my sleeve.

87
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:35,567
Get a flu shot and you won’t catch colds either. Take cold medicine and the cold goes away. We weren’t the only ones who thought so, right?

88
00:07:36,133 --> 00:07:39,533
As we left the clinic, my kid looked up at the building’s signs.

89
00:07:39,600 --> 00:07:46,467
"Dad, is internal medicine for fixing the inside of the body and surgery for the outside? So is a cold internal medicine?"

90
00:07:46,533 --> 00:07:49,567
"...My shot arm hurts. Let’s go."

91
00:07:49,633 --> 00:07:51,533
The next episode is about this question.
